Issue #97: backends: define shared sparse-array contract without silent densification

## Context

Sparse input is a cross-cutting prerequisite for high-dimensional linear models, high-dimensional fixed effects, mixed models, large-scale kernel methods, and several future unsupervised algorithms. Adding ad hoc CSR support to one estimator would create incompatible behavior across SciPy, CuPy, and Torch and could introduce silent densification or device fallback.

Roadmap: PR #89. Multinomial logistic regression is tracked in #96 and should consume this contract only after it is stable.

## Goal

Define and validate a shared sparse-array/backend contract before enabling sparse input in public estimators.

## Supported representations to evaluate

- SciPy CSR and CSC on CPU;
- CuPy CSR and CSC on CUDA;
- Torch sparse CSR where required operations and autograd/solver behavior are viable;
- explicit rejection of unsupported COO/BSR or other formats unless intentionally included.

The issue must distinguish input acceptance from full algorithm support. A backend recognizing a sparse array does not imply that every estimator/solver can operate on it.

## Core contract decisions

### Detection and conversion

- central sparse-array detection helpers;
- explicit conversion rules between CSR and CSC;
- preservation of dtype, device, shape, indices, and sorted/canonical state;
- no conversion to dense unless a public option explicitly requests it and a memory guard approves it;
- no cross-device conversion under explicit `device="cuda"` or `device="torch"`.

### Validation

- finite values in sparse data buffers;
- index dtype and bounds;
- duplicate entries and canonicalization policy;
- zero-size and all-zero matrices;
- feature-count and response alignment;
- sample weights and offsets remain backend-compatible;
- no full GPU-to-CPU copy for routine validation.

### Operation matrix

Document and test which primitives are supported for each backend:

- matrix-vector and matrix-matrix products;
- transpose products;
- column/row scaling;
- reductions;
- slicing and fold indexing;
- intercept handling;
- centering/standardization without densification;
- sparse normal equations;
- sparse linear solves or iterative solvers;
- covariance/inference operations;
- formula-generated sparse design matrices if supported.

### Estimator/solver compatibility

Create an explicit matrix for at least:

- LinearRegression/Ridge;
- Lasso/ElasticNet and FISTA/coordinate-descent paths;
- binary and future multinomial logistic regression;
- GLM IRLS;
- cross-validation fold slicing and final refit;
- panel high-dimensional fixed-effect prerequisites;
- kernel and unsupervised consumers.

Unsupported combinations must fail before fitting with a precise error. They must not silently densify, choose another backend, or run an incomplete objective.

## Memory safety

- configurable or documented dense-memory estimate helper;
- tests proving unsupported densification does not occur;
- OOM/device failures remain fatal and are not converted into generic numerical fallback;
- benchmark peak memory and crossover only after correctness is established;
- large-shape tests may use synthetic sparse structures without allocating the corresponding dense matrix.

## API and sklearn compatibility

- preserve public constructor identity and clone behavior;
- update estimator tags such as sparse-input capability only when the complete public path is supported;
- pipeline, GridSearchCV, nested `set_params`, and transactional refit semantics must remain valid;
- define output type policy for predictions, coefficients, transformed matrices, and summaries.

## Non-goals

- no immediate sparse support for every estimator;
- no sparse GPU performance claim before synchronized benchmark evidence;
- no implicit CPU fallback for missing CuPy/Torch sparse operations;
- no repository-wide solver rewrite;
- no high-dimensional fixed-effect or mixed-model implementation in this issue.

## Required deliverables

1. design document with representation and operation matrix;
2. centralized sparse detection/validation/conversion helpers;
3. no-silent-densification memory guards;
4. backend unit tests for SciPy, CuPy, and Torch sparse arrays;
5. one or more narrowly selected reference estimator paths demonstrating the contract;
6. explicit estimator/solver compatibility documentation;
7. follow-up issues for each estimator family enabled by the contract.

## Required validation

- malformed index/data tests;
- duplicate/canonicalization tests;
- zero and all-zero matrices;
- finite-value validation on all supported backends;
- dtype/device preservation;
- fold slicing and intercept handling;
- proof that rejected paths do not call dense conversion;
- clone/pipeline/GridSearchCV tests for enabled estimators;
- CPU/CuPy/Torch parity for selected reference paths;
- physical GPU memory and correctness smoke tests;
- EN/CN documentation and examples.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] A versioned sparse representation and operation contract is documented.
- [ ] Central helpers detect and validate SciPy/CuPy/Torch sparse arrays without full GPU-to-CPU copies.
- [ ] Silent densification and explicit-device fallback are prevented by tests.
- [ ] An estimator/solver compatibility matrix defines supported and rejected combinations.
- [ ] At least one bounded estimator family exercises the shared contract end to end.
- [ ] Enabled estimators retain sklearn clone, pipeline, CV, and transactional-refit behavior.
- [ ] NumPy/SciPy, CuPy, and Torch physical paths pass maintained validation where declared supported.
- [ ] Follow-up estimator issues reference this contract instead of implementing private sparse adapters.

## Dependencies

This contract should be designed before high-dimensional fixed effects, mixed models, broad sparse linear-model support, or sparse multinomial work.

