Issue #19: Spec: learn vs recompute — adaptive context sufficiency gate

## Problem Statement

PR #18 answered the first Harness-control question with a valid null result: on a tiny state-conditioned benchmark, learned phi matched a strong static Skill/software workflow but did not outperform it. The main control variation was essentially whether repository discovery had already happened, so the result supports a narrower engineering principle:

> **Do not neuralize simple, explicit Harness rules. Neuralize ambiguity, not invariants.**

The next experiment should therefore stop training a general six-action controller and isolate one Harness decision that is both repeated and genuinely uncertain:

> **Is the currently gathered code context sufficient to call the strong reasoner now, or is one more context acquisition worth it?**

This is a direct test of the broader `learn vs recompute` question. A hand-written Harness can use a static context rule. A strong model can recompute the decision JIT on every step. A small learned gate can amortize the decision from prior verified trajectories.

## Research Question

> **Given the same coding tasks, deterministic runtime/verifier, context-access interface, and strong external coding model, can a small learned context-sufficiency gate decide `READ_MORE` vs `REASON_NOW` from observable state/history better than a strong static rule and an LLM/JIT controller, improving time-to-verified-solution without reducing verified success?**

The null hypothesis is important:

> **If a static rule or JIT/LLM control matches or beats the learned gate, then this decision is not worth amortizing into a neural policy on the tested distribution.**

Negative/null results are first-class outcomes.

## Why This Decision

Context acquisition is a stronger candidate for learning than the previous `repo_known -> DISCOVER` branch because:

- it repeats frequently in coding agents;
- the correct decision can depend on several weak signals rather than one Boolean;
- reading too little can cause a bad or premature reasoning call;
- reading too much adds local/tool latency and context volume;
- the result is verifiable through deterministic tests;
- a small local gate can be much cheaper than another serial strong-model control call.

This spec is not about replacing the strong coding model. It is about deciding **when the currently accumulated context is sufficient to spend a strong-model reasoning call**.

## Core Decision Surface

Do not train a new general Harness policy.

At explicit context-sufficiency decision points, the learned component chooses only:

- `READ_MORE`
- `REASON_NOW`

The ordinary deterministic Harness still owns:

- project-root safety;
- filesystem access;
- schema validation;
- patch application;
- test execution;
- exit-code interpretation;
- timeouts;
- mandatory final verification;
- hard run budgets and termination safety.

The existing six high-level Harness actions may remain internally, but the learned experiment must isolate the binary context-sufficiency decision rather than reintroducing a general action classifier.

## Benchmark Requirement: Genuine Context Uncertainty

Build a small frozen deterministic benchmark where context sufficiency genuinely varies.

The benchmark must include cases where:

1. the first relevant read is enough and another read is redundant;
2. one additional dependency/callsite/type-definition read is required before a correct patch can be produced;
3. more than one plausible context source exists and only some are useful;
4. an early `REASON_NOW` can fail verification and force a recovery cycle;
5. a conservative always-read-more workflow succeeds but pays extra actions/context/latency;
6. no single fixed number of reads is perfect on held-out evaluation.

Prefer observable weak signals such as:

- failing-test/error signature;
- symbols referenced by the failure;
- imports/dependency edges;
- files already read;
- unresolved referenced symbols;
- context size;
- previous verification outcome;
- previous reasoner outcome;
- attempt count;
- compact trajectory history.

Do not expose fixture family, expected patch, minimum required reads, oracle action, or any other hidden benchmark label to the policy.

The benchmark should contain multiple trajectory families in train and held-out splits, with disjoint case fingerprints. The old calculator benchmark may remain a smoke test only.

## Conditions

### A. Static context policy

A serious inspectable hand-written Skill/software rule using only observable state/history.

It must be frozen before held-out evaluation and must not be intentionally weak. It may be conservative, but it should react to state rather than replay a fixed path.

### B. Learned context gate

A small local model trained only from training-split canonical trajectories.

Primary candidate: a tiny MLP or similarly lightweight classifier/value gate.

Input must be observable inference-time state/history only.

Output is only `READ_MORE` or `REASON_NOW`.

Held-out data must not affect fitting, threshold tuning, architecture selection, prompt changes, or feature engineering.

### C. LLM/JIT context controller

The same strong external model used for coding reasoning recomputes the same binary context decision autoregressively from the same observable state/history.

This condition must use the same provider/model/version/reasoning settings as the code reasoner and must not receive privileged benchmark information.

### Required ablation: learned linear gate

Include a simple linear/logistic learned gate trained on the same training records.

This is not a primary product condition; it is an attribution control. If a linear gate matches the neural gate, do not claim neural nonlinearity was necessary.

